This is a standard Tietze transformation argument.

## Proof

We have the group presentation
$$G = \langle x_1, \ldots, x_k \mid r_1, \ldots, r_l \rangle$$
where each relator $r_i(x_1,\ldots,x_k)$ can be rewritten as $s_i(x_1,\ldots,x_{k-1}, x_k \cdot w)$ for a word $w = w(x_1,\ldots,x_{k-1})$ in the first $k-1$ generators.

**Step 1: Adjoin the new generator $t$.** Consider the presentation obtained by adding a new generator $t$ together with the defining relation $t = x_k w$:
$$G' = \langle x_1, \ldots, x_k, t \mid r_1, \ldots, r_l,\; t^{-1} x_k w \rangle.$$
This is a Tietze transformation of type I (introducing a new generator defined by a word in the existing generators). Such a transformation produces an isomorphic group, so $G' \cong G$. Concretely, the map $x_i \mapsto x_i$ for $i \le k$ and $t \mapsto x_k w$ gives a homomorphism $G' \to G$ with inverse $x_i \mapsto x_i$ for $i < k$, $x_k \mapsto t w^{-1}$.

**Step 2: Eliminate $x_k$.** In $G'$, the relation $t^{-1} x_k w = 1$ lets us express $x_k = t w^{-1}$, where $w^{-1}$ is a word in $x_1,\ldots,x_{k-1}$. Substituting $x_k = t w^{-1}$ into every relator $r_i$ is a Tietze transformation of type II (eliminating a generator that is expressible in terms of the others). The resulting presentation is
$$G'' = \langle x_1, \ldots, x_{k-1}, t \mid \widetilde{r}_1, \ldots, \widetilde{r}_l \rangle,$$
where $\widetilde{r}_i$ is obtained from $r_i$ by replacing every occurrence of $x_k$ with $t w^{-1}$.

**Step 3: Identify $\widetilde{r}_i$ with $s_i$.** By hypothesis, $r_i$ can be rewritten as $s_i(x_1,\ldots,x_{k-1}, x_k w)$. Under the substitution $t = x_k w$ (equivalently $x_k = t w^{-1}$), the argument $x_k w$ becomes exactly $t$. Therefore
$$\widetilde{r}_i = s_i(x_1, \ldots, x_{k-1}, t).$$
Hence
$$G'' = \langle x_1, \ldots, x_{k-1}, t \mid s_1, \ldots, s_l \rangle.$$

**Step 4: Conclusion.** Both Tietze transformations (introducing $t$ and eliminating $x_k$) preserve the isomorphism type of the group, so
$$\langle x_1, \ldots, x_k \mid r_1, \ldots, r_l \rangle \;\cong\; \langle x_1, \ldots, x_{k-1}, t \mid s_1, \ldots, s_l \rangle.$$

The two groups are **isomorphic**. The substitution $t = x_k w$ (with $w$ a word in $x_1,\ldots,x_{k-1}$) is a Tietze transformation: it replaces the generator $x_k$ by the generator $t = x_k w$, which is invertible since $x_k = t w^{-1}$ is expressible in the remaining generators and $t$. This change of generators is an automorphism of the ambient free group $\langle x_1,\ldots,x_k\rangle$ (a Nielsen transformation), and applying it to all relators simultaneously yields a presentation of the same (isomorphic) group.

### PROOF COMPLETE
